<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rangaku</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rangaku, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> learning, was the body of European scientific and technical knowledge studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> through the Dutch trading post at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dejima">Dejima</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>'s period of restricted foreign contact, Japanese scholars translated and analysed European works on medicine, astronomy and other fields. This knowledge prepared the intellectual ground for the rapid modernisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji period</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rangaku.jpg" alt="Rangaku" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shiba Kōkan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shiba_Kokan_A_meeting_of_Japan_China_and_the_West_late_18th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Rangaku, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> learning, and by extension Yōgaku, Western learning, grew out of contact with the Dutch enclave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dejima">Dejima</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, where the Dutch traders were the only Europeans tolerated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Their movements were watched and strictly controlled. In the first phase Western books were prohibited except those on nautical and medical matters, and a small group of hereditary Japanese-Dutch translators worked in Nagasaki.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> gave the shōgun news of world events and novelties each year on their trip to Edo. The surgeon Caspar Schamberger, one of the most important physicians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dejima">Dejima</a>, induced a continuing interest in medical books, instruments and treatment methods. In 1720 the shōgun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshimune">Tokugawa Yoshimune</a> relaxed the rules, which started an influx of Dutch books and their translation into Japanese.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Medicine was the first field. Debates between proponents of traditional Chinese medicine and the new Western learning led to experiments and dissections, and the New Text on Anatomy of 1774, compiled by scholars led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugita-genpaku">Sugita Genpaku</a> from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> translation of a German work, became a reference. In 1804 Hanaoka Seishū performed a mastectomy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/general-anaesthesia">general anaesthesia</a>, combining Chinese herbal medicine with Western surgery.</p><p>In 1838 Ogata Kōan founded the school Tekijuku, whose alumni included Fukuzawa Yukichi. Shizuki Tadao translated a work on Newton's theories in 1798 and coined terms still in use, such as the words for gravity and centrifugal force. Udagawa Yōan's Opening Principles of Chemistry of 1840 introduced Lavoisier's findings and terms such as oxidation and reduction.</p><p>Other fields followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiraga-gennai">Hiraga Gennai</a> obtained an electrostatic generator from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> around 1770, which the Japanese called the elekiteru and sold as a curiosity. Kunitomo Ikkansai built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s first reflecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescope</a> in 1831. Inō Tadataka made maps between 1800 and 1818 that served as the definitive maps of Japan for nearly a century. In 1824 the German physician Philipp Franz von Siebold began a medical school at Narutaki near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> that grew into a meeting place for about fifty students.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1839 scholars of Western studies briefly suffered repression in the Bansha no goku, after they opposed a measure enacted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> to introduce the death penalty against foreigners other than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> who came ashore. Rangaku became obsolete when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> opened up during the last decades of the Tokugawa regime, as students went abroad and foreign employees came to teach.</p><p>The Nagasaki Naval Training Center, established in 1855 beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dejima">Dejima</a>, was directed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> naval officers until 1859. Rangaku scholars such as Fukuzawa Yukichi, Katsu Kaishū and Sakamoto Ryōma then shifted the main language of learning from Dutch to English, and because they took a pro-Western stance several were assassinated, among them Sakuma Shōzan in 1864 and Sakamoto Ryōma in 1867.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rangaku” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Through the Dutch at Dejima, European science and technology entered Japan as <b>rangaku</b> ('Dutch learning'), and after the shogun Yoshimune relaxed the ban on Western books in 1720, Japanese scholars gained growing access to European astronomy, medicine, physics and military technology within a controlled framework.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
